=== note, on what each block above does and does not show
The ps snapshot at the top is a moment in which four suites stood at once: this worktree's gate suite (pid 2922387,
`--from-git a0ca0f35…`, 33 s at the time), the project tree's own gate suite (pid 1623683, `--from-git 0f366176`,
920 s), and two more `--from-git` runs below them. The refusal that follows is this worktree's second suite, refused
by the lock this worktree's gate suite had taken 33 s earlier — the lock's own file is the line above it.
What this file does NOT show is cross-worktree behaviour: the project tree's suite ran a driver from the project
tree, which in this change's own run predates the lease, so its running beside this one is not evidence that two
*leased* trees do not refuse each other. That is covered where it can be: the third half of
`driver#aSecondSuiteInOneRepositoryIsRefused` runs a suite whose scratch root is its own while the repository's lock
is held, and it runs its cases (see evidence/green-case.log and the full-suite log).
The last block shows what makes that true — the other worktree resolves a scratch root of its own (`.pi/tmp` under
that tree) — and nothing more; the 210 there is that tree's own case count, not a run.
